WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:03.888
Picture moving day.

2
00:00:03.888 --> 00:00:09.420
The truck needs a driver, the driver needs the route, and each packer needs a roll of tape.

3
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.249
The crew can't start until the truck and the packers are ready.

4
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.247
Someone has to write the plan: who comes first, and what is shared.

5
00:00:17.247 --> 00:00:20.923
One truck for the whole day, a new roll of tape for each packer.

6
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:27.080
In Part 4, you built a container that worked this out while the program ran, by reflection.

7
00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.271
That's planning on the street, at seven in the morning.

8
00:00:30.271 --> 00:00:33.404
This time, the build writes the plan the night before.

9
00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:41.190
KSP has already written one card per class: a factory, a small object that builds just that class.

10
00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.499
But no card says when to call it.

11
00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:46.732
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

12
00:00:46.732 --> 00:00:50.236
The plan is a list of lines, one per member of the crew.

13
00:00:50.236 --> 00:00:52.551
Does the order of those lines matter?

14
00:00:52.551 --> 00:00:54.616
Or will the compiler sort it out?

15
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.416
By hand, the plan is the block near main we've all written.

16
00:01:01.416 --> 00:01:02.778
Five lines are easy.

17
00:01:02.778 --> 00:01:03.799
Eighty are not.

18
00:01:03.933 --> 00:01:07.382
Someone adds a parameter to Truck, and the block breaks.

19
00:01:07.382 --> 00:01:11.263
Or worse, it still compiles, and quietly builds a second truck.

20
00:01:11.263 --> 00:01:13.788
Two trucks, blocking the street at seven.

21
00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:18.036
So we'd like the plan written by something that never gets the order wrong.

22
00:01:18.933 --> 00:01:20.972
First, let's draw who needs whom.

23
00:01:20.972 --> 00:01:22.392
Each arrow means needs.

24
00:01:22.392 --> 00:01:25.110
The moving day needs the crew and the route.

25
00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:27.643
The crew needs the truck and two packers.

26
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:31.276
The truck needs the driver, and the driver needs the route.

27
00:01:31.276 --> 00:01:33.573
Each packer needs its own roll of tape.

28
00:01:33.733 --> 00:01:37.198
Gold boxes exist once for the whole day: a singleton.

29
00:01:37.198 --> 00:01:40.074
Purple ones are new every time someone asks.

30
00:01:40.074 --> 00:01:42.820
All together, that's the graph of the day.

31
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.207
How would you write the plan by hand?

32
00:01:45.207 --> 00:01:46.281
You'd phone round.

33
00:01:46.281 --> 00:01:50.279
Call the moving day and ask: what do you need before you can start?

34
00:01:50.279 --> 00:01:52.605
Put it on hold, and call what it needs.

35
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:53.749
Let's map that.

36
00:01:53.749 --> 00:01:56.154
A call is the walk visiting one class.

37
00:01:56.154 --> 00:01:58.939
What do you need is reading its constructor.

38
00:01:58.939 --> 00:02:03.685
Each line on the sheet says how to get one kind of thing: that's a binding.

39
00:02:03.867 --> 00:02:09.164
And one rule: write someone down only when everything they need is already on the sheet.

40
00:02:10.667 --> 00:02:12.702
The walk starts at the moving day.

41
00:02:12.702 --> 00:02:14.797
Which one lands on the sheet first?

42
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.998
The moving day needs the crew.

43
00:02:19.998 --> 00:02:21.663
The crew needs the truck.

44
00:02:21.663 --> 00:02:23.461
The truck needs the driver.

45
00:02:23.461 --> 00:02:25.259
The driver needs the route.

46
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.005
The route needs nothing.

47
00:02:27.005 --> 00:02:28.677
So the route is line one.

48
00:02:28.867 --> 00:02:30.468
Now back up the calls.

49
00:02:30.468 --> 00:02:33.088
The driver has everything: line two.

50
00:02:33.088 --> 00:02:34.689
The truck: line three.

51
00:02:34.867 --> 00:02:38.486
Back at the crew, the first packer needs a tape roll.

52
00:02:38.486 --> 00:02:40.808
The tape roll needs nothing: four.

53
00:02:40.808 --> 00:02:41.969
The packer: five.

54
00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:44.964
The second packer is already on the sheet.

55
00:02:44.964 --> 00:02:46.649
Same line, so we skip it.

56
00:02:46.649 --> 00:02:48.873
Now the crew has everything: six.

57
00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:54.393
Back at the moving day, the route is already on the sheet: shared, not written twice.

58
00:02:54.393 --> 00:02:56.461
And the moving day itself: seven.

59
00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:00.132
Notice: the moving day was asked first, and written last.

60
00:03:00.132 --> 00:03:02.239
The walk never thinks about order.

61
00:03:02.239 --> 00:03:05.833
It just refuses to write anyone down before their helpers.

62
00:03:05.833 --> 00:03:08.125
Stubborn, and that's exactly the job.

63
00:03:08.267 --> 00:03:09.815
That walk has two names.

64
00:03:09.815 --> 00:03:14.458
Depth first: follow one answer all the way down before you try the next.

65
00:03:14.458 --> 00:03:19.167
Post-order: write each one on the way back up, after everything it needs.

66
00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:23.649
In one sentence: a binding joins the plan when the walk leaves it.

67
00:03:24.933 --> 00:03:27.616
Now the processor writes the sheet as Kotlin.

68
00:03:27.616 --> 00:03:30.836
This class is the component: it builds the whole crew.

69
00:03:30.836 --> 00:03:34.532
First the route, then the driver who needs it, then the truck.

70
00:03:34.667 --> 00:03:39.035
The gold ones are vals, in the walk's order: one, two, three, six, seven.

71
00:03:39.035 --> 00:03:41.728
The purple ones became functions, below them.

72
00:03:41.728 --> 00:03:45.737
A function can sit anywhere, because it runs only when it's called.

73
00:03:45.933 --> 00:03:51.546
The crew's line calls newPacker twice, so it gets two packers with two new rolls of tape.

74
00:03:51.546 --> 00:03:54.698
The app prints: one truck for the whole day, true.

75
00:03:54.698 --> 00:03:56.023
Tape rolls made: two.

76
00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:58.435
Now, back to our question.

77
00:03:58.435 --> 00:04:02.208
Say the processor wrote the driver's line above the route's.

78
00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:03.026
What happens?

79
00:04:03.026 --> 00:04:07.365
Does it run anyway, does the driver get null, or does it not compile?

80
00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:11.920
It doesn't compile.

81
00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.783
Kotlin runs these lines top to bottom, like Java's field initializers.

82
00:04:16.783 --> 00:04:23.105
The driver's line reads a route that isn't there yet: Variable 'route' must be initialized.

83
00:04:23.105 --> 00:04:26.371
In Java, javac says: illegal forward reference.

84
00:04:26.533 --> 00:04:31.345
In moving-day terms, Sam is told to drive before anyone has printed the route.

85
00:04:31.345 --> 00:04:34.430
He's not going anywhere, and neither is the build.

86
00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:36.281
So the order isn't decoration.

87
00:04:36.281 --> 00:04:38.379
It's what makes the class compile.

88
00:04:39.267 --> 00:04:40.797
Here's the walk in code.

89
00:04:40.797 --> 00:04:43.603
If the name is already on the sheet, return.

90
00:04:43.603 --> 00:04:47.110
Otherwise visit everything it needs, then add the name.

91
00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:49.405
Java says the same, a little longer.

92
00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:50.934
One caution.

93
00:04:50.934 --> 00:04:54.357
The walk looks at the sheet, but never at who's on hold.

94
00:04:54.357 --> 00:05:02.549
Give it a driver who waits for the truck, and it calls the truck, who calls the driver, who calls the truck, until the stack runs out.

95
00:05:02.549 --> 00:05:04.200
The next lesson fixes that.

96
00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.082
And here the picture breaks.

97
00:05:06.082 --> 00:05:10.228
A real planner can improvise when a neighbour turns up with a ladder.

98
00:05:10.228 --> 00:05:11.609
The build's plan can't.

99
00:05:12.533 --> 00:05:14.986
So, does the order of the lines matter?

100
00:05:14.986 --> 00:05:16.306
It's the whole point.

101
00:05:16.306 --> 00:05:19.702
The walk asks from the top and writes from the bottom.

102
00:05:19.702 --> 00:05:22.091
Shared things become vals, built once.

103
00:05:22.091 --> 00:05:25.047
The rest become functions, built on every call.

104
00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:26.001
Quick check.

105
00:05:26.001 --> 00:05:27.937
Delete @Singleton from Truck.

106
00:05:27.937 --> 00:05:30.873
What does one truck for the whole day print?

107
00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:35.074
It prints false.

108
00:05:35.074 --> 00:05:37.369
The truck becomes a function, newTruck.

109
00:05:37.369 --> 00:05:41.663
The crew got its truck when it was built, and truck() builds another one.
